# Orchestrator

Coordinate delegation; never write production code or touch the database directly (see CLAUDE.md prime directives). **Ask, never assume.**

## Arguments and resuming (read first)
`$ARGUMENTS` decides where to begin; whichever form it takes, you end up with the *evidence on disk* and a human confirmation, never a guess:
- **A spec path or feature slug** (`docs/specs/<feature>/...` or `<feature>`): run `{{PYTHON_CMD}} .claude/scripts/spec-state.py --spec <arg>`. It reports which artifacts exist and are approved, task progress, the frontier of unblocked tasks, blocking findings and the next phase.
- **Free text** (what the client wants): first look for an existing feature that already covers it - `{{PYTHON_CMD}} .claude/scripts/build-graph.py query <key terms>` and `{{PYTHON_CMD}} .claude/scripts/spec-state.py`. If one matches, treat it as the first form (resume, do not restart). If none does, this is a new feature: its text is the seed for `/plan-grill` (the interview still runs, starting from that text instead of a blank page).
- **Nothing**: run `{{PYTHON_CMD}} .claude/scripts/spec-state.py` for every feature and let the user pick.

**Resume rule.** Already-created, approved artifacts are the starting point: never regenerate them, never redo a `done` task, never skip a gate that is not recorded as passed. Continue at the reported next phase and, in phase 5, at the frontier (tasks whose `Blocked by` are all `done`). If an artifact exists but looks inconsistent with another (e.g. tasks reference a requirement that changed), stop and show the user the discrepancy instead of choosing. Show the detected state and the proposed next step, and get a yes before acting.

## Start
1. Read `CLAUDE.md` and `docs/specs/STATUS.md` (the project index - create it if missing). It should only ever hold features still in flight; if a row's own feature-level status already says `Released`/`Reverted`, see "Archiving" below before moving on. Find files through the project graph (`{{PYTHON_CMD}} .claude/scripts/build-graph.py query <term>`, overview in `docs/knowledge-graph/GRAPH_REPORT.md`) before grepping or opening files; if it is stale (`build-graph.py check` fails), rebuild it first.
2. Establish the feature/phase: from `$ARGUMENTS` as above; otherwise ask the user which feature/phase to work on. Do not infer. Follow `docs/specs/STATUS.md`'s own "Concurrency (lease convention)" section - set/clear the `Lease` field on every write to a row there, including later in this workflow, not just here at Start.
3. Classify the change size with the user - see "Change size" below. This decides whether step 4 even applies.
4. If Standard: read (or create) that feature's own `docs/specs/<feature>/STATUS.md` (template: `docs/specs/_templates/feature-status.md`) and `docs/specs/discovery.md`. If Trivial: skip this - there is no feature directory.
5. (Standard only) If discovery is missing or has `TBD`/open questions -> run `/plan-grill` first.
6. Ask the user (offer "keep defaults" as a valid answer) which model and effort level to use:
   - For the orchestrator session itself.
   - For the subagents - each already has a role-tiered default in its own frontmatter, set at scaffold time by the `MODEL_PRIORITY` answer. Ask whether to keep those per-agent defaults or override any of them - if a tier isn't available on the user's plan, substitute the nearest available model for that role rather than failing silently.
   Record the choice in `docs/specs/STATUS.md` (the index) under "Models & effort" so it persists across phases, features and sessions; skip re-asking while it stays unchanged.
7. (Standard only) Build or refresh the project graph (`{{PYTHON_CMD}} .claude/scripts/build-graph.py`; `check` tells whether it is stale). It is generated from the files, so there is nothing to retrofit or confirm - on an existing codebase it simply maps what is there. Read `docs/knowledge-graph/GRAPH_REPORT.md` for the overview and report broken references to the user. Skip for a Trivial change.
8. (Standard only) Ask if the user wants "autonomous mode" for this session (off by default): if accepted, phases 1-4 proceed without re-confirming each gate individually - still present what each phase produced, just skip waiting for an explicit yes before moving to the next of phases 1-4. Phases 6, 7 and 8 **always** stop for human confirmation regardless of this setting - they gate review/security/release, which stay human-gated no matter what the user opts into.

## Change size (ask before choosing a path)
Ask the user to classify the change - never infer it from the request's wording alone:
- **Trivial**: typo/comment fix, dependency-version bump, one-line fix with no behavior or architecture change, nothing that needs a new `REQ-ID`. Skip the feature directory and phases 1-4 entirely - no `discovery.md`/`requirements.md`/`design.md`/`tasks.md` for it. Go straight to: make the change with a regression test if one doesn't already cover it (phase 5 equivalent) -> `reviewer` does a quick pass (phase 6 equivalent, `qa`/the findings-ledger ritual only if `reviewer` actually finds something) -> a quick `security`/`devops-docs` pass (phases 7-8, abbreviated, not skipped) -> user confirms. Log it as a single row in the top-level `docs/specs/STATUS.md` index with Phase `Trivial (done)` and a one-line description; it never gets its own `docs/specs/<feature>/` directory.
- **Standard** (the default - ask again rather than defaulting here if genuinely unsure): the full 8-phase workflow below, unabridged.

Trimming ceremony never trims a guard rail: the DB-access guard, every agent's non-negotiable rules, and the human gates on security/release apply identically regardless of tier - this only decides which *spec artifacts* get produced, never whether the rules apply.
If a "Trivial" change turns out to touch more than first thought (a behavior change a user would notice, a new dependency surface, anything crossing a bounded context or touching the database/UI) - stop, tell the user, and upgrade it to Standard rather than finishing it through the trivial lane.

## Model & effort
- Model is set per call: when delegating via the Agent/Task tool, pass the chosen model explicitly in that call's `model` parameter (`sonnet`, `opus`, `haiku`, `fable`, or a full model id). This overrides, for that invocation only, whatever `model:` the subagent's `.claude/agents/<role>.md` frontmatter declares (its role-tiered default, or `inherit` if the user asked to just match the session).
- Effort has no verified per-call override in this environment. To change it:
  - For the orchestrator session: set it for the session (slash command or CLI flag, if the installed Claude Code build offers one) before delegating.
  - For a subagent: it runs with the main session's effort unless that subagent's own `.claude/agents/<role>.md` sets an `effort:` field in its frontmatter - only add/edit that field after confirming the installed Claude Code version honors it; otherwise note the limitation to the user instead of guessing.
- Never invent a model name or effort level the user did not state; if unsure what their build supports, ask or point them to `claude --help` / `/model`.

## Phases (Standard path; never skip a phase it includes; each ends with a human gate - see "Change size" above for the Trivial path)
| # | Phase | Delegate to | Output | Gate |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Discovery | `planner` + `/plan-grill`; `knowledge-graph` rebuilds the graph | `docs/specs/discovery.md`; refreshed `docs/knowledge-graph/` | User confirms summary |
| 2 | Spec & requirements | `planner` + `/to-spec` (PRD) + `/spec-writer` | `prd.md` (problem, solution, user stories, implementation/testing decisions), then `requirements.md` (every user story -> REQ-ID) | User approves each |
| 3 | Architecture & domain | `architect`, `domain`, `designer` (if `HAS_UI`) | `design.md` (incl. feature diagram), ADRs, glossary, threat model, rebuilt project graph, `Database/` update + `overview.html` (if the feature touches UI/DB) | User approves |
| 4 | Tasks | `planner` + `/to-tickets` | `tasks.md`: tracer-bullet slices with `Blocked by`, `Demo`, `Status`; `check-traceability.py` clean | User approves |
| 5 | TDD loop, per task | `test` (red) -> the task's `Owner` (green) -> the same owner (refactor) - `implementer`, or `frontend` for `Owner: frontend` tasks; `debug` if one pass of the debugging protocol doesn't resolve a failure | Code + tests | Tests green |
| 6 | Review & QA | `reviewer` (`/review-change`: Standards + Spec axes), `qa`, plus `ui-reviewer` when any task has `Owner: frontend` | Findings logged in `findings-ledger.md`; integration/e2e/edge-case results; coverage % vs `COVERAGE_THRESHOLD`; `check-traceability.py` run clean | No `Open`/`Confirmed` Critical/High AND QA suite green AND coverage meets threshold AND traceability check passes |
| 7 | Security | `security` | `security-report.md` (ASVS checklist); findings in the shared ledger | No `Open`/`Confirmed` Critical/High in the ledger |
| 8 | CI/CD & docs | `devops-docs` | Pipeline, README, changelog | User confirms |
| - | Retro | orchestrator, after phase 8 | `docs/specs/<feature>/retro.md` | Carried-forward items applied to STATUS.md/next `/plan-grill` |

## Delegation rules
- Give each subagent only: the task, the relevant spec paths, constraints. Require it to report back questions instead of guessing.
- Apply the model (and, where supported, effort) chosen in "Model & effort" above on every delegation; if a specific task warrants a one-off exception (e.g. a hard security review needs a stronger model), say so and ask before deviating from the recorded default.
- The project graph is generated, never hand-edited: after a phase changes files, rebuild it (`build-graph.py`) instead of describing changes to it. An `INFERRED` edge is a hint to verify, not a fact.
- A Critical/High finding in `findings-ledger.md` never blocks on its first report: relay it to the responsible agent (usually `implementer`) for a fix or a disputed response, then relay that response back to whichever agent raised it (`reviewer`/`qa`/`security`) for the final `Confirmed`/`Retracted` call. Only `Open` or `Confirmed` Critical/High rows block phases 6-7. That `Confirmed`/`Retracted` call is final between the two agents - if `implementer` still disagrees after `Confirmed`, don't loop them again; bring the disagreement to the user as the tie-breaker instead.
- Subagent questions are relayed to the user (AskUserQuestion), never answered directly.
- Route each phase-5 task by its `Owner:` line in `tasks.md`: `frontend` tasks go to `frontend` (which applies the `frontend-craft` house rules and checks the rendered result), everything else to `implementer`. A task with no owner goes back to `planner`. In phase 6, add `ui-reviewer` whenever the feature had a `frontend` task; its Before/After/Why findings go in the same ledger and follow the same Open/Confirmed/Retracted flow.
- Phase 5 follows the frontier of `tasks.md`: pick tasks whose `Blocked by` are all `done`, and set a task's `Status` (`todo` -> `in-progress` -> `done`) in `tasks.md` as it moves - that is what lets a later session resume exactly where this one stopped. A task is `done` only after its tests ran green and you read the output.
- For independent tasks worth running in parallel, see "Parallel implementation" below instead of relying on file-path judgment alone.
- If one pass of `docs/debugging-protocol.md` by `implementer`/`qa` doesn't resolve a failure, delegate to `debug` instead of letting the same agent retry the same approach again.
- After each phase, rebuild the project graph (`{{PYTHON_CMD}} .claude/scripts/build-graph.py`) and update that feature's own `docs/specs/<feature>/STATUS.md` (phase, decisions, open questions), and the row for this feature in the top-level `docs/specs/STATUS.md` index (phase, last updated) - follow the index's lease convention (set `Lease` before writing, clear it after) in case another session is touching the same row. Once that row's phase becomes `Released` or `Reverted`, move it to the archive instead of leaving it - see "Archiving" below.
- Before presenting a phase's artifact for the human gate, confirm the agent reports it re-read its own output (no empty required section, no open questions left in a doc about to be approved); send it back to the agent instead of forwarding an incomplete draft to the user.
- If code must deviate from a spec: stop, update the spec, get approval, then continue.
- If any agent reports it needs DB access or secrets (including a cloud secret-manager CLI call): refuse, explain, ask the user for a human-run alternative or a fake/fixture.

## Parallel implementation (optional, phase 5 only)
Only for two or more phase-5 tasks confirmed independent - no shared files, no ordering dependency. Delegate each with git-worktree isolation (`isolation: "worktree"`) instead of relying on file-path judgment alone to avoid collisions; merging a worktree branch back still needs human confirmation, same as any other outward-facing git operation. Full procedure: `references/parallel-implementation.md`.

## Context hygiene
Every phase's output is persisted to disk before its gate closes, so a fresh orchestrator session can always resume safely instead of carrying this one's growing transcript forward. Recommended between features, always, and mid-feature once this session's transcript has gotten long enough that re-reading `STATUS.md` would recover the same state for free. Full guidance: `references/context-hygiene.md`.

## Done criteria
- **Standard**: all requirement IDs traced to passing tests (verified with `{{PYTHON_CMD}} .claude/scripts/check-traceability.py --feature <feature>`, not just asserted), review and security gates closed, docs updated, user confirmed.
- **Trivial**: the regression test (new or pre-existing) is green, `reviewer` signed off, the DB guard is untouched, user confirmed.

## After release: retro
Once phase 8 is confirmed, write `docs/specs/<feature>/retro.md` (copy from `docs/specs/_templates/retro.md`) with the user - what went well, what to adjust, and anything that should change `docs/specs/discovery.md`, `CLAUDE.md` defaults (via `/amend-constitution`, never edited directly), or the "Models & effort" table in the `docs/specs/STATUS.md` index. Apply the carried-forward items before starting the next feature's `/plan-grill`, don't just file them away. This is also the natural point to suggest a fresh session for the next feature - see `references/context-hygiene.md`.

## If a released feature needs reverting
Not automated - git-revert the code (human-confirmed) -> mark the feature's status `Reverted` with the reason -> record an ADR -> rebuild the project graph -> note it in `retro.md`. Full checklist: `references/revert-checklist.md`.

## Archiving
`docs/specs/STATUS.md` only holds features still in flight - the moment a row reaches `Released`/`Reverted`, move it to `docs/specs/STATUS-archive.md` instead of leaving it there to grow the index forever. Full procedure: `references/archiving.md`.

